\section{Introduction}
\{sec:introduction}

istic models have proven be very useful in a of applications in signal processing where signal estimation is neededcite{rabiner989tutorial,arul02tutorial,ji008esian}. Some of their advantages are that 1) they force the designer to specify all the assumptions of the model,) they provide a clear separation between the model and the algorithm used to solve it,) they usually provide some measure of uncertainty about the estimation.

On the other, adaptive filtering is a standard approach in estimation problems when the input is received as a stream of data that is potentially nonary. This approach is widely understood and applied to several problems as echo cancellationcite{gilloire992adaptive}, noise cancellationcite{nelson991active}, and channelizationcite{falconer2002frequency}.

Although these two approaches share some underlying relations, there are very few connections in the literature. The first important attempt in the signal processing community to relate these two fields was the connection between a linear Gaussian state-space model (i.e.man filter) and the RLS filter, by Sayed and Kailathcite{sayed1994state} and then by Haykin \et al.}{kin997adaptive}. TheLS adaptive filtering algorithm emerges naturally when one defines a particular state-space model (M) and then performs exact inference in that model. This approach was later exploited in \cite{van012kernel} to design a kernelLS algorithm on Gaussian processes.

A first attempt to approximate the LMS filter from aistic perspective was presented incite{park014istic}, focusing on a kernel-based implementation. The algorithm ofcite{park014istic} makes of a Maximum aeriori () estimate as an approximation for the predictive step., this approximation not preserve the estimate of the uncertainty in each step, therefore degrading the performance of the algorithm.

In this work, we provide a similar connection between state-space models and least-mean-squares (MS). Our approach is based onimating the posterior distribution with an isotropic Gaussian distribution. We show how the computation of this approximated posterior leads to a linear-complexity algorithm, comparable to the standardMS. Similar approaches have already been developed for a of problems as channelization using recurrent RBF neural networkscite{cid1994urrent}, or Bayesian forecastingcite{harrison999esian}. Here, we show the usefulness of thisistic approach for adaptive filtering.

Theistic perspective we adopt throughout this work presents two main advantages., a novel LMS algorithm with adaptable step size emerges naturally with this approach, making it suitable for both stationary and nonary environments. The proposed algorithm has less free parameters than previousMS algorithms with variable step sizecite{kwong1992variable,aboulnasr97ust,shin004variable}, and its parameters are to be tuned w.r.t. these algorithms and standardMS., the of aistic model provides us with an of the error variance, which is useful in many applications.

periments with simulated and real data show the advantages of the presented approach with respect to previous works., we remark that the main contribution of this paper is that it opens the door to introduce more Bayesian machine learning techniques, asational inference and Monte Carlo sampling methodscite{barber012esian}, to adaptive filtering.\\


