In this work, in which we on systems of interacting elements,
 the inverse problem concerns  the statistical inference
 of the underling interaction network and of its coupling coefficients from observed data on the dynamics  of the system. 
 Versions of this problem are encountered in physics, biology (.g.,{Balnan11,Ekeberg13,Christoph14}), social sciences and finance (.,{Mastromo12,yamanaka_15}), neuroscience.,{Schneidman06,Roudi09a,tyrcha_13}), to cite few, and are becoming more more important to the increase in the amount of data available from these fields.\\
 \indent 
 A standard approach used in statistical inference is to predict the interactionplings by maximizing the likelihood function.
 This technique,, requires the of the  

   partition function that, in the most general case, concerns a number of computations scaling exponentially with the system size.


   mann machine learning uses Carlo sampling to compute the gradients of the Loglikelihood looking for stationary points \{Murphy12} but this method isationally manageable only for small systems. A of fasterimations, as naive mean-field, independent-pair approximation{R9a,ib}, inversion of TAP equations{Kappen98,Taka98}, small correlations expansion{Sessak09}, adaptiveAP{Opper01}, adaptive  cluster{Cocco12} orheimations{Rci-Tershi12, Nguyen12}  have, then, been developed. These techniques take as input means and correlations of observed variables and most them assume a fully connected graph as underlying connectivity network, or expand  around it by perturbative dilution.  In most cases, network reconstruction turns out to  be not accurate for small data sizes and/or whenplings are  strong or,, if the original interaction network is sparse.\\
indent
 A further method, substantially improving performances for small data, is the so-called Pseudo-Likelyhood MethodM) \cite{Ravikumar10}. \{Aurell12}ll and Eberg performed a comparison betweenM and some of the just mentioned mean-field-based algorithms on the pairwise interactinging-spin  ($\sigma = \pm) model, showing howM performsively better, especially on sparse graphs and in the highpling limit, i.e., for low temperature.

