In the past few, the synthesis ofagneticors has become a major challenge for spinics., growing a magnetic andiconducting material could to promising advances like spin injection into non magneticors, or electrical manipulation of carrier induced magnetism in magneticorscite{ohno00,Bouk02}. Up to now, major efforts have on diluted magneticors (MS) in which the hosticonducting matrix is randomly substituted by transition metal () ions such as Mn, Cr, Ni, Fe or Co{Diet02}. However Curie temperatures ($T_{C}$) inMS remain rather low and TM concentrations must be drastically raised in to increaseC}$ to room temperature. That usually to phase separation and the of secondary phases. It was recently shown that phase separation induced by spinodal decomposition could lead to a significant increase ofC}$cite{Diet06,Fuku06}. Forductors showing $C}$ higher than room temperature one can foresee the fabrication of nanodeices such as memory nanodots, or nanochannels for spin injection. Therefore, the precise control of inhomities appears as a new challenge which may open a way to industrial applications of ferromagnetism inors.

The increasing interest in group-IV magneticors can also be explained by their potential compatibility with the existing silicon technology. In 002, carrier mediatedromism was reported in  MBE grown Ge{.94}$Mn06}$ films by Park \et al.}{Park02}. The maximum critical temperature was 116 K. Recently many publications indicate a significant increase of $}$ in Ge{1-x}$Mn$_}$ material depending on growth conditions \cite{Pint05,Li05,tsui03}. reported aie temperature as 285 K{Cho02}. 
Taking into account the strong tendency of Mn ions to form interlic compounds in germanium, a detailed investigation of the nanoscale structure is required. Up to now, only a few studies have focused on the nancale composition in Ge{}$$_}$ films. \{Kang05} who evidenced a micrometer scale segregation of manganese in large Mn rich stripes. However,ie temperatures never exceed 300 K{Bihl06,Morr06,Pass06,Ahle06}. Ge$_3$n$_5$ clusters exhibit a temperature of 296 K \{Mass90}. This phase frequently observed in Ge$_{1-x}$M$_{x}$ films is the most stable (Ge,Mn) alloy. The other stable compound Ge$_8$n$_{11}$ has also been observed in nanocrallites surrounded with pure germanium{Park01}. Ge$_8$n$_1}$ and Ge$_3$n$_$ phases are feragnetic but their metallic character considerablyates their potential use as spinors.
Recently, some new Mn-rich nanostructures have been evidenced in Ge$_{1}$$_}$ layers. \cite{Sugh05} reported the of high Mn content (between 10 \% and 20 \% of Mn)ous Ge$_{1$_x$itates  in a Mn-free germanium matrix.cite{06} which exhibit aie temperatures below 200 K. Finally, high-$T_{}$ ($>$ 400 K) Mn-rich nanocolumns have been evidenced \{Jame06} which could lead to silicon compatible room temperature operational devices.\\
In the present paper, we investigate the structural and magnetic properties of Ge$_{1-x}$M$_x$ thin films for low growth temperatures ($<$ 20$^{\circ}$C) and low Mn concentrations (between 1 \% and 11 \%). By combining TEM, x-Ray diffraction and SQUID magnetometry, we could identify different magnetic phases. We show that on growth conditions, we obtain either Mn-rich nanocolumns or Ge$_{3}$Mn$_{5}$ clusters embedded in aium matrix. We discuss the structural and magnetic properties of these nanostructures as a of manganese concentration and growth temperature. We also discuss the magneticisropy of nanolumns and  
Ge$_3$Mn$_5$ clusters. 

